The vectors are drawn on a square grid: $\overrightarrow{OA}=\mathbf{a}$, $\overrightarrow{OB}=\mathbf{b}$, and $\overrightarrow{OC}=\mathbf{c}$.

![Vector diagram](vector-09d9cd1ec018040f60c589a9-prompt.png)

Given that $\mathbf{c}=\lambda\mathbf{a}+\mu\mathbf{b}$, find $\lambda$ and $\mu$.

$\lambda=$ {{field-1}}

$\mu=$ {{field-2}}

question.:question/worked-solution:
![Completed vector route](vector-09d9cd1ec018040f60c589a9-solution.png)

Construct the route $O\to X\to C$, first parallel to $\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\mathbf{a}$.

Along $OX$, move $6$ squares right and $3$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $4$ squares right and $2$ squares up. Thus $OX=\frac{3}{2}|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{b}$.

Along $XC$, move $1$ square left and $1$ square down. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square right and $1$ square up. Thus $XC=1|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=-\mathbf{a}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{b}-\mathbf{a}\\&=-\mathbf{a}+\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=-1$ and $\mu=\frac{3}{2}$.

